WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.112
A workspace full of tables
is still a warehouse.

2
00:00:03.112 --> 00:00:06.684
Rules, incidents and automation
make it a detection engine,

3
00:00:06.684 --> 00:00:11.422
and the program's rule is strict: no source
counts as monitored until an enabled

4
00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:16.087
rule reads it, and no rule counts until
its output reaches a queue someone owns.

5
00:00:16.087 --> 00:00:19.212
The engine also sets a
floor on time to detect.

6
00:00:19.212 --> 00:00:22.309
A scheduled rule runs
every five minutes at best,

7
00:00:22.309 --> 00:00:24.787
with a five-minute delay for ingestion.

8
00:00:24.787 --> 00:00:28.802
A near-real-time rule runs every
minute on a two-minute delay,

9
00:00:28.802 --> 00:00:31.588
and a workspace can
enable only 50 of them.

10
00:00:31.588 --> 00:00:34.324
Behavioral baselines are slower by design.

11
00:00:34.324 --> 00:00:40.263
Defender XDR detects what its own sensors
see on endpoints, identities and email.

12
00:00:40.263 --> 00:00:47.075
Sentinel rules cover what Defender can't
see: DNS, SASE, SQL audit and other clouds.

13
00:00:47.075 --> 00:00:51.473
Then prepare for the assessor's test:
show an alert from the last 30 days,

14
00:00:51.473 --> 00:00:53.975
the incident it opened and what was done.

15
00:00:53.975 --> 00:00:59.540
That takes four records: the alert, the
ticket, the response notes and the closure.
